44AM MST until November 2 at 7:00PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall is expected.\n\n* WHERE...South Central Archuleta County in southwestern Colorado...\n\n* WHEN...Until 700 PM MST Monday.\n\n* IMPACTS...Flooding of rivers, creeks, streams, and other low-lying\nand flood-prone locations is imminent or occurring.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 944 AM MST, satellite estimates and rain gauge data indicate\nheavy rain in the warned area. Flooding is ongoing or expected to\nbegin shortly.\n- This includes the following streams and drainages...\nArroyo Escondido, Montezuma Creek, Navajo River, Cat Creek, San\nJuan River and Blanco, Rio.\n- Some locations that will experience flooding include...\nGato, Juanita and Pagosa Junction.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T19:00:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.c47213b8846f19b1cb8b737423603b76e5d64476.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.c47213b8846f19b1cb8b737423603b76e5d64476.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Warning","headline":"Flood Warning issued November 1 at 9:30AM MST until November 2 at 7:04PM MST by NWS Grand Junction CO","description":"...The Flood Warning continues for the following rivers in\nColorado...\n\nSan Juan River at Pagosa Springs affecting Archuleta County.\n\n.Significant and historic flooding is possible along the San Juan\nRiver at Pagosa Springs. Widespread rainfall is expected to begin\nearly Sunday morning and continue through Monday morning. Excessive\nrunoff will also increase flooding risks. The region should prepare\nfor a dangerous and impactful flood event.\n\nFor the San Juan River...including Pagosa Springs...major flooding is\nforecast. This is a particularly dangerous situation.\n\n* WHAT...Major flooding caused by excessive rainfall is forecast\nalong the San Juan River. This is a Particularly Dangerous\nSituation.\n\n* WHERE...San Juan River at Pagosa Springs.\n\n* WHEN...Until Monday evening.\n\n* IMPACTS...At 20.0 feet, Access roads to the San Juan River Ranch\nare flooded. Some homes are inaccessible.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- At 9:00 AM MST Sunday the stage was 7.8 feet.\n- Bankfull stage is 8.5 feet.\n- Forecast...The river is expected to rise above flood stage this\nafternoon to a crest of 22.0 feet this evening. It will then fall\nbelow flood stage early tomorrow afternoon.\n- Flood stage is 10.5 feet.\n- Flood History...This crest compares to a previous crest of 17.8\nfeet on 10/05/1911.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood","ends":"2026-11-02T19:04:00-07:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Archuleta, CO","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.f943f0ea1c90f489e1f4da4d4300877d56242bd0.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.f943f0ea1c90f489e1f4da4d4300877d56242bd0.001.1"}],"afd":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-02T23:47:00+00:00","snippet":"000 FXUS65 KGJT 022347 AFDGJT Area Forecast Discussion National Weather Service Grand Junction CO 447 PM MST Mon Nov 2 2026 .KEY MESSAGES... - Rivers across southwest CO and southeast UT remain rain swollen due to runoff. The San Juan River at Pagosa Springs remains in major flood stage. - Light rain below 10000 feet will continue, with snow above. Winter weather highlights continue for the mounta","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=AFD&format=CI"},"hwo":null,"fwf":{"office":"GJT","issued":"2026-11-02T18:59:00+00:00","snippet":".DISCUSSION... This is an automatically generated forecast with no discussion. The normal Fire Weather Forecast will resume in its entirety in the spring. ...GUSTY ERRATIC WINDS CAN BE EXPECTED NEAR ANY SHOWERS OR THUNDERSTORMS... ...THE SMOKE DATA IS CALCULATED FROM AN AVERAGE AT 6000 FEET MSL... COZ200-022300- Little Snake Forecast Area- 1159 AM MST Mon Nov 2 2026","url":"https://forecast.weather.gov/product.php?site=GJT&issuedby=GJT&product=FWF&format=CI"}